// groom-work-item — the two-phase groom cut's phase graph.
//
// The ratified clause this graph realizes is SPECIFICATION/contracts.md
// §"Grooming and slice-size calibration" -> "Consensus-gated automated groom
// cut". One run consumes ONE work-item and runs exactly ONE of two phases:
//
//   PROPOSE — draft the decomposition exactly as the `groom` front-end drafts
//   it, file NOTHING, and terminate at a needs-human outcome carrying the
//   draft. The Dispatcher journals that termination and records the draft on
//   the item as a ledger comment; the item rests at blocked / needs-human.
//
//   APPLY — produce the FILING PLAN for the approved cut, file nothing, and
//   terminate carrying that plan. It runs only on a later dispatch of the same
//   item whose rendered goal carries the recorded approval. The Dispatcher
//   reads the plan off the terminated run and performs the filing HOST-SIDE.
//
// WHY THE APPLY PHASE DOES NOT FILE, which is the one place this graph departs
// from the obvious reading of the ratified clause. A factory sandbox is
// deliberately given NO ledger credential -- the run-scoped environment carries
// the model and forge tokens and nothing else, and the credential wrapper a
// `bd` call falls back to is a HOST path absent from the container -- so an
// apply node that filed would reach for a secret this sandbox is designed never
// to hold, and no retry could change that. Measured 2026-09-07 on run
// 01M1XNN6C8WHCW4EGDC7CWK6WW: implement, conformance, review and phase_gate all
// succeeded and the filing node died on the missing credential with the ledger
// untouched. The clause's MUSTs constrain WHAT is filed and HOW, never WHERE
// the call executes, and the same clause already assigns a ledger write to the
// Dispatcher on the propose side. Projecting the tenant password into a groom
// sandbox was the alternative and is rejected: it hands a tenant-write
// credential to an agent-driven container to save a seam the Dispatcher has.
//
// THE PHASE IS DERIVED FROM THE RENDERED GOAL, NEVER FROM AN INPUT. The
// Dispatcher folds the item's ledger comments into the goal verbatim, so the
// drafting comment and the operator's approval answer are both IN the brief
// every ACP node reads. The `implement` node reads them and decides; it writes
// the decision to a sandbox scratch file so the two script nodes below can
// route on it. That is what keeps this variant's declared input token set
// identical to the reserved workflow's -- a phase input would have been a
// token the bundle does not declare, and the peer clause forbids the
// divergence.
//
// NEVER A WAITING RUN. Neither phase parks. The propose phase's product is a
// dead run plus a ledger comment, and the apply phase's is a dead run plus a
// filed cut -- which is the only shape §"A factory run never awaits a human"
// permits. Neither phase ends green, and neither reaches the `exit` node:
// a groom run publishes to the LEDGER and never to the forge, so its whole
// product is a ledger effect the Dispatcher performs from the run's account.
//
// PEER PARITY. The six ACP node names below are the reserved workflow's six,
// because §"Self-contained plugin dispatch" -> "A registered variant is the
// reserved workflow's peer, not its exception" requires the per-repository
// adapter layer, the `dispatcher.codex_models` expansion and the per-node
// timeout table to resolve against this graph without naming an absent node. A
// variant differs in its EDGES, its retry and review discipline, its prompts,
// its run configuration and its sandbox image -- never in those six names. So
// `pr` here is the apply phase's publication node with the LEDGER as its
// publication surface, and `fix` repairs a malformed draft rather than a red
// check suite. (Keep brace tokens out of comments BEFORE the digraph line --
// a brace there breaks Fabro's graph render in the web UI.)
digraph GroomWorkItem {
    // Retry discipline mirrors the reserved workflow's: single-shot by
    // default, one explicit retry on the two goal-gated nodes, and the engine's
    // own failure-category awareness decides which failures spend it
    // (transient infrastructure faults re-execute; deterministic ones fail
    // straight through the `outcome=failed` edges to the human gate).
    //
    // Every duration here is a DEFAULT the Dispatcher rewrites from the
    // target's `dispatcher.node_timeouts` table before `fabro run` sees the
    // payload. The values match the resolution's own defaults so a direct
    // `fabro run` of this file behaves like an unconfigured dispatch. Do NOT
    // template a `timeout` from a workflow input: the pinned build types a
    // quoted duration at parse time and never re-types a rendered string, so a
    // templated timeout leaves the node with NO timeout and reports nothing.
    graph [
        goal="Draft or apply one work-item's decomposition per the ratified two-phase groom cut"
        default_max_retries=0
        stall_timeout="7200s"
    ]

    start [shape=Mdiamond, label="Start"]
    // Declared as this graph's terminal MARKER, and deliberately unreachable:
    // see "NEVER A WAITING RUN" above. Both phases end at `needs_human`, which
    // is the one channel MEASURED to carry a node's own output through to
    // `fabro inspect` -- and therefore the only one the Dispatcher can read a
    // draft or a filing plan off. A green ending would route this run into the
    // pull-request and merge-confirmation path, which a groom run has no pull
    // request for.
    exit  [shape=Msquare, label="Exit"]

    // The groom node. It derives the phase from the rendered goal, then either
    // drafts the cut (propose) or prepares the filing plan (apply). It writes
    // NOTHING to the ledger in either phase -- the apply phase's filing is the
    // `pr` node's job, which is what makes "the propose phase files nothing" a
    // structural property of the graph rather than a promise in a prompt: the
    // propose phase never reaches the node that can file.
    implement [
        label="Groom: derive the phase, then draft the cut or plan the filing"
        backend="acp"
        acp.command="{{ inputs.implement_adapter }}"
        timeout="1800s"
        max_retries=1
        goal_gate=true
        retry_target="implement"
        prompt="@prompts/implement.md"
    ]

    // The groom run's own hard gate, and the reason it is NOT the repository's
    // declared check suite. A groom run changes no tracked file, so the suite
    // would verify an untouched tree at the cost of a full run of it; what
    // actually needs proving about a groom run is the opposite fact -- that the
    // tree really is untouched -- plus the shape of what the groom node
    // produced. Both are cheap and mechanical, so they are asserted here rather
    // than delegated:
    //
    //   1. the phase file names exactly one of the two phases;
    //   2. the working tree is byte-identical to the dispatch base, which is
    //      the machine-checkable form of "a groom run files nothing into the
    //      repository". The dispatch base is the governed repository's DEFAULT
    //      BRANCH, rendered from the resolved contract rather than spelled;
    //   3. the phase's PRODUCT -- the draft in the propose phase, the filing
    //      plan in the apply phase -- exists, is exactly ONE non-empty line,
    //      and carries no brace character.
    //
    // The one-line rule is not style. The Dispatcher recovers both products
    // from the terminated run's needs-human sentinel, which is a stderr LINE,
    // and it reads the text following the sentinel ON THAT LINE -- so a product
    // spanning several lines would arrive truncated to its first, SILENTLY.
    // It binds the apply phase for the sharper reason: a truncated PLAN is a
    // cut the Dispatcher would file with slices missing, which no later
    // dispatch can undo. The brace rule is the same kind of hazard one step
    // further on: a ledger comment is append-only, and a comment carrying a
    // template opening delimiter poisons every future goal render for that
    // item permanently. The Dispatcher refuses such a draft rather than
    // escaping it; this node refuses it earlier, while the run can still be
    // sent back to `fix` to re-encode it.
    conformance [
        label="Conformance: phase named, tree untouched, product single-line and brace-free"
        shape=parallelogram
        timeout="600s"
        script="phase=$(cat /tmp/livespec-groom-phase 2>/dev/null || true); if test \"$phase\" != propose && test \"$phase\" != apply; then echo 'LIVESPEC_GROOM_MALFORMED: the groom node named no phase; expected propose or apply in /tmp/livespec-groom-phase' >&2; exit 1; fi; if ! git diff --quiet origin/{{ inputs.default_branch }}...HEAD; then echo 'LIVESPEC_GROOM_MALFORMED: the working tree differs from dispatch base origin/{{ inputs.default_branch }}; a groom run files into the ledger and must change no tracked file' >&2; exit 1; fi; if test \"$phase\" = apply; then product=/tmp/livespec-groom-plan; else product=/tmp/livespec-groom-draft; fi; if ! test -s \"$product\"; then echo \"LIVESPEC_GROOM_MALFORMED: the $phase phase produced nothing at $product\" >&2; exit 1; fi; if test \"$(wc -l < \"$product\")\" -gt 1; then echo \"LIVESPEC_GROOM_MALFORMED: $product spans more than one line; only its first line would reach the Dispatcher\" >&2; exit 1; fi; if grep -q '{' \"$product\"; then echo \"LIVESPEC_GROOM_MALFORMED: $product carries a brace, which would poison every future goal render of this item\" >&2; exit 1; fi; if test \"$phase\" = apply && ! grep -q '^livespec-groom-plan' \"$product\"; then echo 'LIVESPEC_GROOM_MALFORMED: the filing plan does not open with the livespec-groom-plan marker, so the Dispatcher would read it as a draft and revoke the approval' >&2; exit 1; fi; exit 0"
    ]

    // Repairs a draft or filing plan the conformance gate refused. Mirrors the
    // reserved workflow's `fix` node -- implementer adapter, re-enters the gate
    // it failed -- and differs only in what it is repairing.
    fix [
        label="Fix conformance failures"
        backend="acp"
        acp.command="{{ inputs.fix_adapter }}"
        timeout="1800s"
        max_visits=3
        prompt="@prompts/fix.md"
    ]

    // The review gate, applied to a CUT rather than to a diff. It reads the
    // draft or the filing plan and asks the questions the maintainer would ask
    // of a decomposition: is each slice one coherent, autonomously-verifiable
    // done; are the dependency layers right; is a spec-change slice routed to
    // the propose-change path rather than to the factory. Deliberately NO
    // max_visits: the review<->review_fix loop is bounded GRACEFULLY by the
    // edge guard below, and a hard visit ceiling would abort on the very visit
    // that must route onward.
    review [
        label="Review the cut: coherence, verifiability, layering, routing"
        backend="acp"
        acp.command="{{ inputs.review_adapter }}"
        timeout="1800s"
        max_retries=1
        prompt="@prompts/review.md"
    ]

    // Read-only triage of the reviewer's BLOCKING findings before any rework,
    // exactly as in the reserved workflow: accept in-scope correct findings,
    // reject out-of-scope, not-applicable, factually wrong or scope-expanding
    // ones with a one-line rationale. `max_visits` is the ungraceful
    // runaway-loop backstop and MUST exceed the largest supported
    // `review_fix_visit_cap` override; the graceful bound is the review edge
    // guard.
    disposition [
        label="Disposition review findings"
        backend="acp"
        acp.command="{{ inputs.disposition_adapter }}"
        timeout="1800s"
        max_retries=1
        max_visits=10
        prompt="@prompts/disposition.md"
    ]

    // Revises the draft or the filing plan for ONLY those findings the
    // disposition record marks ACCEPTED, then re-enters the conformance gate --
    // a revision can perfectly well break the single-line or brace-free rule,
    // so it must be re-proved rather than assumed.
    review_fix [
        label="Revise the cut for accepted findings"
        backend="acp"
        acp.command="{{ inputs.review_fix_adapter }}"
        timeout="1800s"
        max_visits=10
        prompt="@prompts/review-fix.md"
    ]

    // WHICH PHASE PUBLISHES. Reached only once the cut has been reviewed and
    // approved, this is the fork between the two endings, and it is a SCRIPT
    // node on purpose: the phase was decided once, by the node that read the
    // goal, and re-deriving it here from a second agent's reading is exactly
    // how the two could come to disagree about which phase this run is. Exit 0
    // routes the apply phase to the filing node; any other exit routes the
    // propose phase to the needs-human terminal, which carries the draft.
    phase_gate [
        label="Which phase publishes: apply files, propose parks"
        shape=parallelogram
        timeout="600s"
        script="phase=$(cat /tmp/livespec-groom-phase 2>/dev/null || true); if test \"$phase\" = apply; then exit 0; fi; echo 'LIVESPEC_GROOM_PROPOSE: propose phase complete; parking the draft for a human decision without filing anything' >&2; exit 1"
    ]

    // The apply phase's publication node, and what it publishes is the FILING
    // PLAN -- not the slices. It re-reads the reviewed plan, takes the
    // approving invoker's identity and the approval route off the answer
    // comment, and writes the finished one-line plan back to
    // /tmp/livespec-groom-plan for the terminal below to carry. The ledger
    // write itself is the Dispatcher's, host-side, for the credential reason
    // stated at the top of this file. It carries the same goal gate and single
    // retry the reserved workflow's publish node carries.
    pr [
        label="Publish the filing plan for the Dispatcher to file host-side"
        backend="acp"
        acp.command="{{ inputs.pr_adapter }}"
        timeout="1800s"
        max_retries=1
        goal_gate=true
        retry_target="pr"
        prompt="@prompts/pr.md"
    ]

    // The TERMINAL, and BOTH phases' normal ending. It is the same dead-end
    // shape the reserved workflow's terminal is: it preserves the tree on a
    // run-scoped ref best-effort, writes the LIVESPEC_NEEDS_HUMAN sentinel to
    // stderr, and exits 1 with no outgoing edge, so the run terminates
    // non-green.
    //
    // What differs between the phases is the sentinel's PAYLOAD, and the
    // Dispatcher tells them apart by the payload's own leading marker. The
    // apply phase's FILING PLAN is preferred when present, because an apply run
    // may also have left the propose phase's draft file behind from an earlier
    // read; the propose phase never writes a plan, so the order is
    // unambiguous in both directions. The Dispatcher records a draft as the
    // ledger comment the apply phase will later read, and FILES a plan
    // host-side. Either is emitted through `head -n 1` so a product that
    // somehow reached here spanning several lines is truncated HERE, where the
    // truncation is visible in the run record, rather than silently inside the
    // Dispatcher's line-scoped reader.
    //
    // The ref is derived ONLY from FABRO_RUN_ID with no placeholder fallback:
    // the pinned fabro exports that variable to hooks and not to script nodes,
    // and a defaulted name silently collapses every run onto one shared ref.
    needs_human [
        label="Terminate: carry the draft or the filing plan to the Dispatcher, preserve the tree"
        shape=parallelogram
        timeout="600s"
        script="if test -n \"${FABRO_RUN_ID:-}\"; then ref=\"refs/heads/needs-human/$FABRO_RUN_ID\"; git add -A >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; git -c user.name=fabro -c user.email=fabro@livespec.invalid commit -q -m 'needs-human: preserve tree before terminating' >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; if git push origin \"HEAD:$ref\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo \"LIVESPEC_NEEDS_HUMAN_PRESERVED: $ref\" >&2; else echo 'LIVESPEC_NEEDS_HUMAN_PUSH_FAILED: tree not pushed; rely on the preserve-by-reference dump pointer' >&2; fi; else echo 'LIVESPEC_NEEDS_HUMAN_NO_RUN_ID: no FABRO_RUN_ID in this script environment, so no run-scoped ref exists to push and nothing was pushed; rely on the preserve-by-reference dump pointer' >&2; fi; if test -s /tmp/livespec-groom-plan; then echo \"LIVESPEC_NEEDS_HUMAN: $(head -n 1 /tmp/livespec-groom-plan)\" >&2; elif test -s /tmp/livespec-groom-draft; then echo \"LIVESPEC_NEEDS_HUMAN: $(head -n 1 /tmp/livespec-groom-draft)\" >&2; else echo 'LIVESPEC_NEEDS_HUMAN: the groom run could not auto-resolve this work-item and produced neither a draft nor a filing plan; run terminated, decision routed to the ledger (blocked / needs-human)' >&2; fi; exit 1"
    ]

    // Terminal non-converged node, the conformance loop's cap. Same sentinel
    // and same Dispatcher consumer as the reserved workflow's: a groom run that
    // cannot produce a conformant draft after its budget of repairs is an
    // item the loop should stop spending on, and the Dispatcher marks it
    // needs-regroom rather than retrying it forever.
    non_converged [
        label="Non-converged: conformance loop cap hit; route back to Dispatcher (needs-regroom)"
        shape=parallelogram
        timeout="600s"
        script="echo 'LIVESPEC_NON_CONVERGED: groom conformance loop cap hit without converging; routing back to the Dispatcher for needs-regroom' >&2; exit 1"
    ]

    start -> implement

    // A failed groom node routes to the terminal; everything else is proved
    // conformant before any review turn is spent on it.
    implement -> needs_human [label="Blocked", condition="outcome=failed", weight=100]
    implement -> conformance

    // Conformance routing. The cap is enforced as a visit guard on the GATE's
    // own visit count, not by `fix`'s max_visits alone: a node that reaches
    // max_visits aborts the whole run at entry rather than emitting a routable
    // failure, so no edge could carry the exhaustion onward. Gate visit N means
    // N-1 completed repairs; `< 3` allows two, then the third failure falls
    // through to the non-converged terminal. `fix`'s own max_visits stays as
    // the abort backstop.
    conformance -> review [label="Conformant", condition="outcome=succeeded"]
    conformance -> fix [label="Malformed", condition="outcome!=succeeded && context.internal.node_visit_count < 3"]
    conformance -> non_converged [label="Exhausted (conformance loop cap)"]
    fix -> conformance

    // Review routing. `review_fix_visit_cap` is one larger than the configured
    // fix-round cap because the guard counts review VISITS including the first
    // review, so the default cap of three rounds renders as `< 4`.
    //
    // NO SHIP-ON-CAP ESCAPE HATCH, deliberately, and this is where the groom
    // variant's review discipline departs from the reserved workflow's. The
    // bundle may ship a still-objected-to diff at the cap because a merged PR
    // is revisable. A filed cut is not comparably cheap to undo -- it becomes
    // the backlog other work is planned against -- so a cut the reviewer still
    // objects to parks for a human instead.
    review -> needs_human [label="Blocked", condition="outcome=failed", weight=100]
    review -> disposition [label="fix", condition="preferred_label=fix && context.internal.node_visit_count < {{ inputs.review_fix_visit_cap }}"]
    review -> phase_gate [label="approve", condition="preferred_label=approve"]
    review -> needs_human [label="unmatched review outcome"]

    disposition -> needs_human [label="Blocked", condition="outcome=failed", weight=100]
    disposition -> review_fix [label="fix accepted", condition="preferred_label=fix"]
    disposition -> review     [label="all rejected", condition="preferred_label=all_rejected"]
    disposition -> needs_human [label="unmatched disposition outcome"]
    review_fix -> conformance

    // The two endings. Apply publishes its filing plan; propose parks with its
    // draft. Both reach the same terminal, because both hand their product to
    // the Dispatcher over that terminal's sentinel.
    phase_gate -> pr [label="apply", condition="outcome=succeeded"]
    phase_gate -> needs_human [label="propose"]

    // Unconditional: a `pr` node that could not publish a plan terminates
    // carrying whatever it left behind, and one that DID publish terminates
    // carrying the plan. The Dispatcher discriminates on the payload, and a
    // plan it cannot parse is journaled as a refusal with the item left where
    // the ordinary needs-human escalation rests it.
    pr -> needs_human [label="Plan published; the Dispatcher files it host-side"]
}
